(Abstract) Tj
T*
(We conducted a multisite randomized trial of a digital lifestyle intervention designed to) Tj
T*
(improve metabolic health and outcomes in subsequent pregnancies. Women recruited in the) Tj
T*
(immediate postpartum period were randomized at 6 weeks to receive either structured) Tj
T*
(digital coaching combined with mobile health monitoring \(n = 187\) or standard postpartum) Tj
T*
(care \(n = 191\). The intervention was delivered across the interpregnancy period and during) Tj
T*
(the subsequent pregnancy. The primary composite outcome \(any occurrence of pregnancy-) Tj
T*
(induced hypertension, gestational diabetes mellitus, caesarean delivery, or delivery of a) Tj
T*
(large-for-gestational-age infant\) occurred in 34% of the intervention group compared to) Tj
T*
(47% in the control group \(adjusted odds ratio 0.59, 95% CI 0.41-0.85\). Mean gestational) Tj
T*
(weight gain was significantly lower in the intervention group \(8.1 kg vs 10.4 kg, p =) Tj
T*
(0.002\). Participant engagement with the application and coaching sessions was variable;) Tj
T*
(approximately 71% completed at least 75% of coaching sessions. Weight loss in the) Tj
T*
(interpregnancy period predicted better subsequent pregnancy outcomes. The intervention was) Tj
T*
(generally well-tolerated with high satisfaction ratings \(85% reported satisfaction or high) Tj
T*
(satisfaction\). This multisite study provides evidence for digital lifestyle interventions) Tj
T*
(in preventing adverse pregnancy outcomes.) Tj
